    "tiles": [
      {
        "id": "c-qysfELvytIFe45",
        "title": "Investigate",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p>You already made a prosthesis that can be controlled by a Dataflow program. Now, we want to make it <em>bionic</em> - we want it to respond to the user&#x27;s own body, just like a hand would.</p><p>So, we want it close when the user flexes their muscles, and open when they relax their muscles.</p><p>Electromyography (EMG) measures the electrical activity of muscles. Your spiker:bit (the white and orange part attached to the black microcontroller you have) can measure EMG signals!</p><p></p><p>To connect the EMG</p><p></p><p></p><p>First, we need to figure out how the spiker:bit works with Dataflow. You also need to design ...</p><p></p><p>THINK ABOUT WHERE THE USER MIGHT WANT (AND BE ABLE TO USE) THE ELECTRODES.</p><p>THINK ABOUT THEIR LIMB DIFFERENCE, COMFORT, AND EFFECACY.</p><p></p><p></p><p><strong>Overview</strong></p><p>Today we will explore how the nervous system processes information about touch.  </p><p><strong>Learning Objectives</strong></p><p>By the end of the lesson you will be able to describe why different body parts have different sensitivity to touch.</p><p><strong>Do Now: </strong>Drag this tile into your workspace and answer the following questions </p><ol><li>Which body parts have the highest touch sensitivity? Why?</li><li> Do you think that Tilly can feel objects with her bionic arm?</li></ol>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "9t_w8rwy7Z2carhg",
        "title": "Connecting the EMG reader to your body",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p>To use the EMG reader, you need to connect three electrodes to your body (the little stickers).</p><p>Two electrodes go on the muscle that you are measuring. They connect to the red wires.</p><p></p><p>The third one connects to the black wire, which is called the &quot;ground wire.&quot; It tells your microcontroller what electrical signals are moving through your body normally.  It goes somewhere nearby, but NOT on a muscle area.</p><p>For example, if you are measuring your forearm, you can put the electrode that connects to the ground wire on the back of your hand or on your wrist.</p><p>If you are measuring your blinking, you can put the electrode that connects to the ground wire behind your ear.</p>"
          ]
        }
      },
